WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.240
Film Courage: We talked about text, subtext
and context.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:04.610
What does each item mean?

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:08.510
Adam Skelter, Story Artist and Writer: Well
that's a big subject.

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:17.480
Text, subtext and context...so ultimately
it is about a character wanting something,

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:18.480
right?

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.000
Every story is about a character wanting something
and then facing a conflict to get it whether

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.760
they get it or not.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:31.890
So when they want something they have a desire.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.680
That desire is expressed through subtext.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.489
If I want this glass of water I'm going to
take the action right?

00:00:38.489 --> 00:00:43.829
So the subtext is that you can infer I’m
thirsty or I'm looking for some sort of like

00:00:43.829 --> 00:00:47.600
physical distraction to keep my hands busy
or something like that.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.930
[Takes a drink of water and gulps].

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:52.310
Film Courage: And the gulping is part of that,
too.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:53.310
Adam: Sure.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:56.829
Film Courage: Maybe I'm about to confront
you on something or I'm your

00:00:56.829 --> 00:01:00.149
boss or you know whatever, I'm an ex-girlfriend…there’s
just so many

00:01:00.149 --> 00:01:03.870
things and you’re like “Gulp…what’s
she going to say to me?”

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:04.870
Adam: Exactly.

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:09.280
So a lot of that is about interpreting, we
are guessing what a character wants from the

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.189
behaviors they are exhibiting right.

00:01:13.189 --> 00:01:19.060
Now the context is simply the worldview, the
situation they’re dealing with.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:26.530
So we’re sitting in a room right now and
we all have different objectives.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:34.259
So the context is literally just like plate
that you are serving everything on.

00:01:34.259 --> 00:01:39.890
Like visually I came up with the metaphor
of creme brulee.

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:43.649
I just used a visual where the plate is the
context.

00:01:43.649 --> 00:01:51.880
And then you have the creme as the subtext
and the top layer that is burned and scorched

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:56.579
(bruleed) is the text.

00:01:56.579 --> 00:02:01.539
So the idea is basically what we’re seeing
is words right?

00:02:01.539 --> 00:02:07.670
And those words are a complete contradiction
of actual intention but somehow we are interpreting

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:11.330
those words as a kind of behavior.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:16.800
So most of the time when I’m writing the
last thing I do is write dialogue.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.040
That was another thing I learned from Ron
Mita.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:24.300
You want to be able to…well…do it however
you want.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:29.970
But for me I like to have the characters intentions
very clear and for the conflict that the are

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:32.070
going to be facing very clear.

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:37.410
And what we’re constantly trying to do…there
is this one book called CHARACTER AND VIEWPOINT

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:47.000
where it talks about how essentially we are
all like chimpanzees in the wild and when

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:53.060
they are in the jungle and they sense that
there is a threat, they run away.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:57.460
And the first thing they do (as soon as they
feel like they’re safe) the very first thing

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:02.690
is they stop, they turn around to see where
the source of danger is so that’s when they

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:04.280
are completely engaged.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:09.460
The whole chimpanzee metaphor is basically
that we are trying to interpret the things

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:10.720
that are around us.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:16.030
We were are projecting what that person wants
by the way it’s behaving.

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:22.050
So we are trying to determine whether it’s
a threat or on our side or against us (that

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:23.280
kind of thing).

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:30.710
So ultimately with dialogue is we are trying
to see if we can believe them.

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:36.000
And the most interesting characters are completely
unaware of what is actually driving them.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:48.960
Like the whole time Indiana Jones is going
through these ridiculous hoops not really…the

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.450
whole time he’s doing this we’re like
why is he taking on these, why is he being

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:57.200
dragged by a chair just so something can make
it into a museum?

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:02.180
Ultimately he has this stuff…it depends
on how far you want to go with Indiana Jones

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:09.610
but ultimately he has this drive that is genuinely
emotionally upsetting to him that there would

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:16.850
be some betrayal, some misrepresentation of
truth and that’s what’s interesting and

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:21.030
so the whole time he is making these really
bold choices (I think he is sort of this glib,

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:30.480
sarcastic professor, but what he’s really
doing is he’s trying to unearth the truth

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:35.980
and he’s literally putting his body through
all this brutality because he wants the truth

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:38.480
to be revealed.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:46.980
And you know that because every single choice
(whether he admits it or not) has led to toward

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:48.890
exposing the truth for what it is.

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:54.860
Film Courage: So which is better subtext or
context?

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:56.870
Because we know the less you tell…

00:04:56.870 --> 00:05:01.130
Adam: That’s like saying which is better
the trigger or the bullet?

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:03.259
Film Courage: Okay.

00:05:03.259 --> 00:05:08.370
Which is better to focus on?

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:09.450
Because text I get.

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:11.050
Subtext, yes I get.

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:16.730
Because you said that when you went to take
the drink you’re thirsty or maybe you’re

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:17.920
using it as a buffer…

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:20.980
Adam: Subtext is an expression of the desire.

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:26.330
And the text is…it’s only one means of
expressing that desire.

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:27.330
Film Courage: Sure.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:28.580
Instead saying “I’m thirsty!”

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:31.580
And then by doing it [picking up a glass to
drink water] it’s much more believable or

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:34.250
I’m realizing that you’re needing that
glass for something.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:39.160
But then the context is where I’m just trying
to figure that out a little bit more?

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:40.530
So the context would be…

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:52.380
Adam: Okay so for example we will take a simple
line “Go ahead, drink it.”

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:53.380
Right?

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:56.780
So “Go ahead, drink it” is the text.

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:59.710
We can add some different subtext to it.

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:02.890
So if I say “Go ahead, drink it.”

00:06:02.890 --> 00:06:08.720
[Says it in a sinister tone] There’s a different
subtext maybe more threatening.

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:09.720
“Go ahead!

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:10.720
Drink it!”

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:13.060
I’m really saying “I don’t really care
what you do.”

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:16.320
If I say “Go ahead…drink it!”

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:20.220
It just takes a little bit but the subtext
is totally different.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:21.930
Well I changed the text a little bit.

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:24.010
So that’s text and subtext.

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:36.380
The context is we are sitting in a bus and
I’m holding a and you’re holding poison

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:39.330
and I’m holding your child.

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:44.889
So now the context is extremely loaded and
the choice that you’re about to make is

00:06:44.889 --> 00:06:51.520
implicit and I’m saying “If you don’t
drink it, I’m going to your baby!”

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:53.420
So I say “Go ahead…drink it.”

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:57.780
So the context is the story.

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:00.630
The context is the situation we are dealing
with.

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:03.840
You can come up with a much better scenario
but that’s what comes off the top of my

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:04.840
head.

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:13.169
But the idea is the text is ironically because
writers tend to overemphasize dialogue so

00:07:13.169 --> 00:07:16.090
much the text tends to get too much importance.

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:20.000
The most important stuff is the context and
the subtext.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:24.300
Because the subtext is the intention.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:27.590
The context is the conflict that you’re
trying to deal with (the conflict that you’re

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:29.440
trying to deal with).

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:36.530
So if we’re in space and we’re flying
toward the moon…

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:41.120
Film Courage: Is this GRAVITY by the way?

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:42.120
Adam: I don’t know?

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:43.440
I’m just making this up as we go.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:48.199
If we’re flying toward the moon and we know
that the only thing that is going to save

00:07:48.199 --> 00:07:57.690
us is this little serum and it could either
boil our blood or it could save our lives

00:07:57.690 --> 00:08:01.970
and we’ve just given up and we’ve been
through this whole journey and we’re about

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:06.200
to open the hatch and I say “Go ahead, drink
it.”

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:11.210
Then we open the hatch, it totally changes
the whole context.

00:08:11.210 --> 00:08:14.170
So context is about the situation you’re
dealing with.

00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:15.500
The subtext is intentional.

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:19.820
The text is the art of facts, it’s just
one intention of that desire.

